   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_9wdm8dH4bQQbc97k",
   "name": "Trigonometric Proof",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Trigonometric proof"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Proving trigonometric identities"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- You can use trigonometric identities you already know to prove new identities\n- Make sure you know the **simple trigonometric identities** and **further trigonometric identities**\n\n  - $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ=1$\n  - $\\frac{\\mathrm{sin}θ}{\\mathrm{cos}θ}=\\mathrm{tan}θ$\n  - $1+\\mathrm{tan}^{2}θ=\\mathrm{sec}^{2}θ$\n  - $1+\\mathrm{cot}^{2}θ=\\mathrm{cosec}^{2}θ$\n- To prove an identity start on one side and proceed step by step until you get to the other side\n\n  - e.g. to prove that `open parentheses sin theta plus cos theta close parentheses squared equals 1 plus 2 sin theta cos theta`\n  - Start with the left hand side, and expand\n  \n    - $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ+2\\mathrm{sin}θ\\mathrm{cos}θ$\n  - Simplify using the identity $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ=1$\n  \n    - $1+2\\mathrm{sin}θ\\mathrm{cos}θ$\n  - This is now equal to the expression on the right, so the statement has been proven"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Make sure you are confident handling fractions and fractions-within-fractions"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/07/5.8.1-Trig-Proof-Illustr-3.png",
   "file": "assets/cc0faa3198b7-5-8-1-trig-proof-illustr-3.png",
   "alt": "Example including a fraction within a fraction, and cot",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Always keep an eye on the 'target' expression – this can help suggest what identities to use"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Don't forget that you can start a proof from either side – sometimes it might be easier to start with a particular side\n\n  - If you get stuck - try starting from the other side instead!"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Prove that:\n\n`sec squared space x open parentheses cot squared space x minus cos squared space x close parentheses equals cot to the power of 2 space end exponent x`\n\n> *Use the definitions of *$\\mathrm{sec}x=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{cos}x}$* and *$\\mathrm{cot}x=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{tan}x}=\\frac{\\mathrm{cos}x}{\\mathrm{sin}x}$*, to rewrite the left hand side*\n\n`fraction numerator 1 over denominator cos squared italic space x end fraction open parentheses fraction numerator cos squared space x over denominator sin squared space x end fraction space minus space cos squared space x close parentheses`\n\n> *Expand the brackets, using the fact that multiplying by *$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{cos}^{2}x}$* is the same as dividing by *$\\mathrm{cos}^{2}x$\n\n$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{sin}^{2}x}-1$\n\n> *Use the definition of *$\\mathrm{cosec}x=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{sin}x}$* to rewrite the expression*\n\n$\\mathrm{cosec}^{2}x-1$\n\n> *Use the identity *$1+\\mathrm{cot}^{2}x=\\mathrm{cosec}^{2}x$* to rewrite the expression*\n\n$1+\\mathrm{cot}^{2}x-1$\n\n> *Simplify to achieve the right hand side*\n\n$\\mathrm{cot}^{2}x$"
  }
 ]
}